WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.221
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online English for Academic Purposes.

2
00:00:06.521 --> 00:00:09.838
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.138 --> 00:00:16.110
Unit fifteen: Argument and Opinion. Today you will argue a view with reasons and careful language.

4
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:19.016
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:23.130
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:24.822
Argument.

7
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:28.199
Argument means a set of reasons for a view.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:30.435
Build a strong argument.

9
00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.551
Your turn. Say: Argument.

10
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:37.122
Argument.

11
00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:39.082
Counterargument.

12
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:42.536
Counterargument means a view that disagrees.

13
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.766
Answer the counterargument.

14
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.444
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

15
00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.487
Counterargument.

16
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.239
Persuade.

17
00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:57.459
Persuade means to make someone agree.

18
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:59.318
Persuade the reader.

19
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:02.546
Your turn. Say: Persuade.

20
00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:06.355
Persuade.

21
00:01:07.117 --> 00:01:07.967
Valid.

22
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:11.099
Valid means reasonable and sound.

23
00:01:11.683 --> 00:01:13.137
That is a valid point.

24
00:01:13.750 --> 00:01:16.426
Your turn. Say: Valid.

25
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:20.059
Valid.

26
00:01:20.817 --> 00:01:21.702
Bias.

27
00:01:22.717 --> 00:01:24.844
Bias means unfair preference.

28
00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:26.950
Avoid bias.

29
00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.493
Your turn. Say: Bias.

30
00:01:33.333 --> 00:01:34.404
Bias.

31
00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:36.325
Justify.

32
00:01:37.317 --> 00:01:39.514
Justify means to give reasons for.

33
00:01:40.117 --> 00:01:41.687
Justify your opinion.

34
00:01:42.283 --> 00:01:44.692
Your turn. Say: Justify.

35
00:01:47.533 --> 00:01:48.662
Justify.

36
00:01:49.417 --> 00:01:50.418
Convincing.

37
00:01:51.417 --> 00:01:53.544
Convincing means able to persuade.

38
00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:55.395
A convincing reason.

39
00:01:55.983 --> 00:01:58.543
Your turn. Say: Convincing.

40
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.366
Convincing.

41
00:02:03.117 --> 00:02:04.060
Rebut.

42
00:02:05.050 --> 00:02:07.166
Rebut means to argue against.

43
00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:08.902
Rebut the claim.

44
00:02:09.517 --> 00:02:12.552
Your turn. Say: Rebut.

45
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:16.424
Rebut.

46
00:02:17.233 --> 00:02:20.907
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:24.121
Visal, how do I argue in an essay?

48
00:02:25.217 --> 00:02:36.368
State your position in the thesis, give two or three strong reasons with evidence and then consider the opposite view. Finally explain why your view is stronger.

49
00:02:37.483 --> 00:02:39.030
Can I write I think?

50
00:02:40.117 --> 00:02:50.661
In academic writing it is better to use impersonal phrases, such as it can be argued that or the evidence suggests that, with careful hedging.

51
00:02:51.750 --> 00:02:53.239
What is a counterargument?

52
00:02:54.350 --> 00:03:02.285
It is a reason against your view. Mention it fairly, for example Some people argue that, and then rebut it with better evidence.

53
00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:04.794
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:05.650 --> 00:03:08.973
Grammar focus: Hedging and impersonal phrases.

55
00:03:09.273 --> 00:03:10.599
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:14.316
It can be argued that online study is more flexible.

57
00:03:15.217 --> 00:03:18.342
The evidence suggests that sleep improves results.

58
00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:27.664
Use phrases such as it can be argued that and the evidence suggests that. Add words such as may, tends to and often to avoid absolute claims.

59
00:03:28.417 --> 00:03:31.696
Be careful! Don't say: I thinks it is true.

60
00:03:33.633 --> 00:03:35.993
Say: It can be argued that it is true.

61
00:03:36.293 --> 00:03:38.141
Use an impersonal phrase.

62
00:03:38.933 --> 00:03:41.319
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:42.133 --> 00:03:43.390
Taking a Position.

64
00:03:43.690 --> 00:03:46.316
Piseth must argue for or against homework.

65
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:49.567
He states his position in the thesis.

66
00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:53.576
He gives two reasons and supports them with evidence.

67
00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.926
Then he mentions the opposite view fairly.

68
00:03:57.933 --> 00:04:00.246
He rebuts it and writes a firm conclusion.

69
00:04:01.333 --> 00:04:05.672
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:06.517 --> 00:04:08.760
A counterargument is the blank view.

71
00:04:09.060 --> 00:04:12.400
A, opposite. B, same. C, empty.

72
00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:17.747
The answer is A: opposite.

73
00:04:18.883 --> 00:04:20.302
Valid means blank.

74
00:04:20.602 --> 00:04:23.791
A, loud. B, reasonable. C, funny.

75
00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.530
The answer is B: reasonable.

76
00:04:30.683 --> 00:04:32.671
Bias is an blank preference.

77
00:04:32.971 --> 00:04:36.729
A, equal. B, exact. C, unfair.

78
00:04:40.267 --> 00:04:42.336
The answer is C: unfair.

79
00:04:43.483 --> 00:04:45.274
To rebut is to argue blank.

80
00:04:45.574 --> 00:04:48.809
A, against. B, with. C, late.

81
00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:54.471
The answer is A: against.

82
00:04:55.617 --> 00:04:57.767
Piseth supports reasons with blank.

83
00:04:58.067 --> 00:05:01.256
A, luck. B, evidence. C, jokes.

84
00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.206
The answer is B: evidence.

85
00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.737
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:12.583 --> 00:05:14.223
How do you argue in an essay?

87
00:05:23.017 --> 00:05:24.436
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:24.736 --> 00:05:35.703
I state my position in the thesis and give strong reasons with evidence. I mention the opposite view fairly and rebut it. I use careful phrases such as it can be argued that.

89
00:05:36.003 --> 00:05:37.620
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:38.517 --> 00:05:43.387
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hedging and impersonal phrases.

91
00:05:43.687 --> 00:05:48.591
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

92
00:05:48.891 --> 00:05:51.427
See you in Unit sixteen. Goodbye!

93
00:05:51.727 --> 00:05:54.333
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

